=== READING ===
ownership-metadata passes everywhere: every Helm-applied object carries
managed-by=Helm + meta.helm.sh/release-{name,namespace}, so a GitOps manifest that
reproduces them WILL adopt rather than recreate. That was the main unknown.

Two real blockers remain, and neither is a code problem:

1. stateful-resources — loki (StatefulSet + PVC storage-loki-0), tempo
   (StatefulSet) and pa (PVC pa-platform-agent-store) hold data. A botched
   adoption there is data loss, not an outage. kind has no CSI snapshotter by
   default, so a snapshot mechanism has to exist before those three move.

2. source-reachable — the local branch is ahead of origin, and the engine syncs
   the REMOTE. Adopting now would apply older chart content; the resulting churn
   would look exactly like an adoption failure and send the next person hunting
   the wrong bug. Pushing is an explicit user gate, so this stays blocked.

rollouts-demo is the one release with no data at risk: it clears every check
except the unpushed-source one, making it the natural first subject once the
branch is pushed.

=== FALSE POSITIVE FIXED DURING THIS RUN ===
The first version judged Pods/ReplicaSets too and reported "4 resources would not
be adopted" for loki. Those are controller-created descendants that never carry
Helm ownership — filtering on ownerReferences turned a false BLOCKED into a
clean pass (12 resources). A checker that cries wolf gets ignored.
